1,Create a Go program that reads environment variables to configure an HTTP client using the `os` package. Set up the client with timeouts and proxy settings based on the input variables. Perform a GET request to a specified URL with the configured client and print the response body to the console.,807,,"['Here is a basic example of how you might implement this in Go:

```go
package main

import (
	""fmt""
	""io/ioutil""
	""net/http""
	""net/url""
	""os""
	""time""
)

func main() {
	// Read environment variables for configuration
	timeout := os.Getenv(""TIMEOUT"") // Timeout in seconds
	proxyURL := os.Getenv(""PROXY_URL"") 
	requestURL := os.Getenv(""REQUEST_URL"")

	if timeout == """" || proxyURL == """" || requestURL == """" {
		fmt.Println(""One or more environment variables are not set."")
		return
	}

	// Parse the timeout and create a http client with it
	duration, err := time.ParseDuration(timeout + ""s"")
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Printf(""Invalid timeout: %v\n"", err)
		return
	}

	proxyURI, err := url.Parse(proxyURL)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Printf(""Failed to parse proxy URL: %v\n"", err)
		return
	}

	// Create a HTTP client with the specified timeout and proxy settings
	client := &http.Client{
		Transport: &http.Transport{
			Proxy: http.ProxyURL(proxyURI),
		},
		Timeout: duration,
	}

	// Perform a GET request to the specified URL
	resp, err := client.Get(requestURL)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Printf(""Failed to make GET request: %v\n"", err)
		return
	}

	defer resp.Body.Close()

	// Read the response body
	body, err := ioutil.ReadAll(resp.Body)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Printf(""Failed to read response body: %v\n"", err)
		return
	}

	// Print out the response body
	fmt.Println(string(body))
}
```
This code first reads three environment variables: `TIMEOUT`, `PROXY_URL`, and `REQUEST_URL`. It then creates an HTTP client with the specified timeout and proxy settings. After that, it performs a GET request to the URL stored in `REQUEST_URL` using the configured client and prints out the response body.
